Task 2: Drain Nodes Using oc adm drain
Subtask 2.1: Understand Node Draining Process
Node draining is a critical operation that safely removes workloads from a node before maintenance. For OpenShift Virtualization, this process includes special handling for VMs.

Review drain command options:
# Display drain command help
oc adm drain --help | grep -A 10 -B 5 "virtual"
Check current node schedulability:
# Verify node is currently schedulable
oc get node $MAINTENANCE_NODE -o jsonpath='{.spec.unschedulable}'
echo ""
Subtask 2.2: Perform Node Drain Operation
Initiate node drain with VM-specific options:
# Drain the node with appropriate flags for VMs
oc adm drain $MAINTENANCE_NODE \
  --ignore-daemonsets \
  --delete-emptydir-data \
  --force \
  --timeout=300s

# Alternative command for environments with live migration
oc adm drain $MAINTENANCE_NODE \
  --ignore-daemonsets \
  --delete-emptydir-data \
  --disable-eviction \
  --timeout=600s
Monitor the drain process:
# Watch node status during drain
watch -n 5 "oc get node $MAINTENANCE_NODE -o custom-columns=NAME:.metadata.name,STATUS:.status.conditions[-1].type,SCHEDULABLE:.spec.unschedulable,TAINTS:.spec.taints"
Create a monitoring script for drain progress:
cat << 'EOF' > monitor-drain.sh
#!/bin/bash
NODE=$1
if [ -z "$NODE" ]; then
    echo "Usage: $0 <node-name>"
    exit 1
fi

echo "Monitoring drain progress for node: $NODE"
echo "========================================"

while true; do
    echo "$(date): Checking node status..."
    
    # Check if node is unschedulable
    UNSCHEDULABLE=$(oc get node $NODE -o jsonpath='{.spec.unschedulable}')
    echo "Node unschedulable: $UNSCHEDULABLE"
    
    # Count remaining pods
    POD_COUNT=$(oc get pods -A --field-selector spec.nodeName=$NODE --no-headers 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
    echo "Remaining pods: $POD_COUNT"
    
    # Count remaining VMs
    VM_COUNT=$(oc get vmi -A --field-selector spec.nodeName=$NODE --no-headers 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
    echo "Remaining VMs: $VM_COUNT"
    
    if [ "$VM_COUNT" -eq 0 ] && [ "$POD_COUNT" -eq 0 ]; then
        echo "Node successfully drained!"
        break
    fi
    
    echo "Waiting 30 seconds..."
    echo "------------------------"
    sleep 30
done
EOF

chmod +x monitor-drain.sh
Run the monitoring script:
# Monitor drain progress
./monitor-drain.sh $MAINTENANCE_NODE
